/-!
# Reflections — runnable proof-engineering demonstrations

This library collects the self-contained (core Lean, no `L4YAML` import) runnable
illustrations of the proof-engineering principles documented as numbered *Reflections* in
`Blueprint/08-initiative-4-intrinsic-foundations.md`.  They are kept separate from the
behavioural L4YAML test suites (parsing/emitting/round-trip) because they assert *methodology*,
not library behaviour: each is a toy model whose `#guard`/`#guard_msgs`/`#eval`/`by decide`
checks fail the build if the principle's positive/negative witnesses ever drift.

Each module names the Blueprint Reflection it illustrates:
The list below is grouped by the same themes as the Blueprint's Reflections
section, numerically ascending within each theme.  It is PARTIAL -- only the
`import` lines above are load-bearing, so a demo without a bullet is not a defect.

## Lean 4 mechanics & build hygiene

### Tactics & elaboration (Reflections 592, 593, 596, 611, 613)

* `LoopPushPointwise`                    — Reflection 592 (element-value join for `parseFlow{Sequence,Mapping}Loop_push_pointwise` (SS5.10.5). For each index `j < result.1.size` the loop makes at least one sub-call step that PUSHES `v = result.1[j]!` into the accumulator, with pre- and post-push sub-call witnesses bracketing the push. Relies on R591's indexed sub-call witness from `parseFlowSequenceLoop_step_subwit`. Axiom set `[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`.)
* `ScalarTokenPinned`                    — Reflection 593 (scalar token content pinning (SS5.12). `scanDoubleQuoted_tokens_push_content`: when the scanner input is `'"' :: escapeString content ++ '"' :: rest` in flow context, `scanDoubleQuoted` pushes exactly `.scalar content .doubleQuoted` -- content pinned, no existential. Derived by combining the existing `exists c` push (`scanDoubleQuoted_tokens_push`) with the `lastRealTokenVal?` pinning from `scanDoubleQuoted_flow_ok` and `YamlToken.scalar.inj`. `scanNextToken_flow_scalar_filtered_push_content` lifts this to the `scanNextToken` level. Axiom set `[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]` plus `native_decide` axioms from `collectDoubleQuotedLoop_escapeString_succeeds` and its escape-character dependencies.)
* `ScannerSpanLocality`                  — Reflection 596 (scanner-span-locality body-content fact for all-scalar flow sequences (Front-B, SS5.12). `emitList_allScalar_body_content_at`: for a non-empty all-scalar flow sequence `emitList items` scanned from a flow-context state, produces a body block with `block.length = 2 * items.length - 1` and `block[2*j]!.val = .scalar items[j].content .doubleQuoted` at each even index. Proof by direct list induction parallel to `emitList_scans_safebody` in BlockProducers.lean: `scanNextToken_flow_scalar_filtered_push_content` pins the scalar head token; `scanNextToken_flow_comma_filtered_push` + `scanNextToken_preprocess_flow_ws1` absorb the `", "` separator; the IH provides the tail block and the indexing hops use `List.getElem!_cons_succ` twice to skip the two prefix tokens. Associativity fix: `rest_1 := ',' :: ...` named as a `let` binding so that `h_chars ▸ hcorr` produces `hcorr_h : ScannerSurfCorr s (esc ++ rest_1)` with `rest_1` as an opaque parameter -- avoids the `++` left-vs-right associativity mismatch that `rw` at `hcorr` would leave. Axiom set: R593's set (propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound, plus native_decide axioms from double-quoted scanner and escape functions).)
* `CasesReordersTelescope`                  — Reflection 611 (`rename_i x₁ … xₙ` renames the last `n` inaccessible hypotheses **in CONTEXT order**, and `cases` does NOT introduce a constructor's fields in DECLARATION order — it floats binders that nothing depends on ahead of the ones that do. A `rename_i` list written by reading the constructor therefore binds the wrong hypotheses, and the failure surfaces downstream as an "application type mismatch" against a SIBLING field's type, never as a naming complaint at the `rename_i` itself. Toy `Frame.held (a b c d) (h : Entries a b) (hcl : Closeable h) (hcomma : Comma b c) (hsep : Sep c d)` models L4YAML's `SeqFrame.betweenHeld`: `hcl` is DEPENDENT (it mentions the proof `h`, not just its indices), `hcomma` is position-only, and `cases` floats `hcomma` ahead of `h` while the `h`/`hcl` dependency block moves as a unit — so declared `hentries, hcl, hcomma, hsep` becomes context `hcomma, hentries, hcl, hsep`. `order_probe` is the METHOD: a `trace_state` in the arm whose output is PINNED by `#guard_msgs`, so a future reshuffle fails this file rather than some consumer's `rename_i`. Three arms on one goal: `via_cases_with` (STABLE — `cases h with | held …` binds by constructor field order, immune to the permutation, preferred for arms you expect to edit), `via_rename_i_context_order` (correct list, from the probe), and `rename_i_declaration_order_binds_swapped` (THE TRAP — the declaration-order list still elaborates, but `hcl` is what carries `Entries a b`, so the proof term that type-checks reads as nonsense). `same_names_different_order` + `dependency_block_stays_adjacent` (`by decide`) make the permutation a decidable fact: the two orders are permutations, so COUNTING fields never detects it and no arity check fires; index-determined fields (`a`, `d`, the `Tail`) are substituted away, so the 8 declared binders yield 6 inaccessibles. Diagnose by tracing, never by reading the constructor. All three arms axiom-FREE)
* `DoEarlyExitDuplicatesContinuation`    — Reflection 613 (a statement-style early-exit guard `if cond then throw e` inside a `do` block does NOT elaborate to an `ite` over the rest of the block: Lean wraps the continuation in a JOIN POINT, `have __do_jp := fun __r => <rest>`, and branches to it from both sides. One guard, one layer; a SECOND consecutive guard nests a second layer AROUND the first. That nesting is what proofs are coupled to and it is invisible in the source. Three consequences, each demonstrated: (1) `split at h` on the two-layer shape descends into the join point and splits the INNER guard first, leaving `h` textually unchanged — it does not error, it silently produces goals the script did not anticipate; (2) `simp` normalizing the hypothesis zeta-expands a layer and inlines `<rest>` at BOTH branch references, so k layers give 2^k copies — with a real dispatcher arm that is how `split`'s internal `simp` hits *maximum number of steps exceeded*, a SIZE failure distinct from the OPACITY failure the same construct causes when `rw [if_pos]`/`split` cannot see the arm at all (DOCS item 9a); (3) the fix is NOT "convert to an `else` chain" — that re-shapes the arm so downstream scripts break too — but to add ZERO new guards, folding the new rejection into the existing one via a helper returning `Option Err` checked with `if let some e := … then throw e`. A baseline arm (`oneGuard`), the THREE ways to add a second rejection to it (`twoGuards`, `elseDo`, `merged`, proved behaviourally identical by a `#guard` sweep so the choice is visibly only about shape) and a growth probe (`threeGuards`, a genuinely different function, present only to show the layers nesting one per guard). `invert_merged` is `invert_one`'s script VERBATIM; `invert_two` needs a `by_cases` enumeration instead. **Count the `if`s in the arm before adding one — that count, not the guard's logic, is the interface.** DOUBLES AS A TOOLCHAIN CANARY: SS4 pins the elaborated term of every shape with `#guard_msgs in #print`, and SS3 pins the tactic behaviour with `fail_if_success`, so a Lean bump that moves `do`-desugaring or `split`'s reach into join points fails HERE rather than as diffuse breakage across the scanner-inversion proofs. Axioms `[propext]`. L4YAML DOCS item 9c, 2026-08-07: the indexed block-scalar arm already had two guards, a third blew the step limit in `Proofs/Scanner/IndexedDispatch.lean`, the `else do` rewrite moved the failure rather than removing it, and folding 9c's check into the existing header check as `blockScalarPreErrIx` needed ZERO proof edits in that file)

## Porting, modularization & increment economics

### Splitting, modularization & increment sizing (Reflections 352)

