* `BackwardLocatorMirrorsForward`           — Reflection 309 (a backward locator mirrored from a forward one needs NO standalone backward-matching-open primitive, and its DELIVERABLE is sized to the consumer's reads — the toy of `flowBracketBalance_backward_open_locate`, the HARD CORE of the `(i'-b-backward-locator)` descent. Tokens are abstracted to a delta stream `d : Nat → Int` (each delta in `{-1,0,1}`); `bal d n` is the running balance from `0`. **Correction 1 (POSITIVE `backwardLocate`)**: the locator `∃ p, p < a ∧ d p = 1 ∧ bal d (p+1) = bal d a` is proved by `Nat.strongRecOn` on `a`, casing the last delta `d (a-1)` into opener/neutral/closer; the closer case does NOT author a standalone backward-matching-open lemma (the mirror of a forward matching-close scan) — the IH at `a-1` IS the closer's matching opener `p'`, and a one-line `pairSkip` (`bal d a = bal d p'`, toy of `flowBracketBalance_bracket_pair_skip`) jumps the matched block, then the IH at `p'` walks outward. The recursion structure IS the backward matching-open; mirroring a locator backward SHEDS machinery ([[ref-near-leaf-mirror-sheds-machinery]] on the matched-block-skip axis), it does not add it. **Correction 2 (NEGATIVE, `#guard`-backed)**: the deliverable carries NO Dyck FLOOR, even though the real forward lemma has a symmetric one — the consumer (`Located`/the FROM-LOCATED assembler) reads only `loS := p+1`, `loS ≤ a`, `bal d loS = bal d a` (`locatorAssembles` builds it in one line). On `[ ] [` (`dd = [1,-1,1]`, `bal dd 3 = 1`) the pruned existential is satisfied by BOTH the true innermost opener `p=2` AND a SPURIOUS outer opener `p=0` whose floor dips negative — `#guard floorOK dd 3 3 == true` vs `#guard floorOK dd 1 3 == false`; the floor would separate them, but the consumer needs neither innermost-ness nor the floor, only `bal d loS = bal d a`, so dropping the floor (≈ half the proof: the closer case's floor-extension across the skipped block) loses nothing the assembler consumes. The loc