## Probing, de-risking & refutation

### Probe before producing (Reflections 297, 303, 313, 339, 340, 468, 612, 614, 615, 616, 617, 618, 622, 625, 638)

* `MinimalPairExtractsTheGate`              — Reflection 297 (probe a gated `∀`-over-sub-windows as a MINIMAL PAIR; the gate is read off, not invented — the toy of the `BodySuccSeqDiscriminator` concrete-emitter probe. When a deferred universal is FALSE in general but you suspect a gate rescues it, do not probe a single (holding) instance — probe a minimal pair inside one object: one window that HOLDS the property and a sibling that FAILS it. The object `[ { k : v } , x ]` (`tok`/`stream`) holds a seq interior `[1,8)` and a map interior `[2,5)`. POSITIVE `seq_window_holds ∧ seq_window_is_seq_typed`: `succHolds 1 8 = true` (toy of `bodySucc` — the nested map is invisible, balance `> 0` there) and the gate classifies it `enclosingMark 0 = some true`. NEGATIVE `map_window_fails ∧ map_window_is_map_typed`: `succHolds 2 5 = false` (the key at `k=2` is a depth-`0` end followed by `colon`, not a `comma` separator) and the gate classifies it `enclosingMark 1 = some false`. The probe does triple duty: confirms the gate SUFFICIENT (holding window), proves it NECESSARY (failing sibling), and NAMES it as the existing typed-stack-top computation (`stFold`/`enclosingMark`, toy of `WellTyped`/`btFold`: `seqOpen ↦ true`, `mapOpen ↦ false`) that SEPARATES the pair. The lesson: the structural feature that differs between the holding and failing windows IS the discriminator — read it off the pair, do not invent it)
